A||||19||Cheever3000||Minivan smokes at idle.  Why?  ||||11||
Z||000000||Cheever3000||10-03-2001||11:56 AM||||Got a 94 Plymouth Grand Voyager, 6-cyl, 106000 miles.<BR>It doesn't smoke at startup, but it does at idle, like at a drive-thru, train crossing, long red light, etc etc.  It seems to be getting worse, and at times can be embarrassing.  Sometimes have to turn it off so as not to bother the people around us.  My wife says sometimes when she gets out it "smells hot".<P>What's causing this, and is there something I can do to help keep it running maybe another 12 months?  <BR>||||reg||11
Z||000001||Joe Torma||10-03-2001||12:12 PM||||Well, at least there will be no mosquitos within 10 yards of your van.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>How's your PCV valve?||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroMaster88||10-03-2001||12:21 PM||||Well..the 3.0L engines have a problem with valve seals.  they go bad all the time.  That is probably your problem.  Dono what to suggest about fixes.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">              <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||10-03-2001||12:22 PM||||Does the transmission have a vacuum modulator? <BR>At idle, the manifold vacuum is at maximum. If the modulator has a punctured diaphragm, tranny fluid will be sucked out, and burned in the engine. Will be white smoke. Probably lots of it.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||falconhulk||10-03-2001||01:26 PM||||You did not say what color the smoke was or where it was coming from.  Thats very important.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dumba$$||10-03-2001||02:27 PM||||its a dodge they all smoke  every last one of them||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cheever3000||10-03-2001||02:31 PM||||It's from the tailpipe.<BR>I'll follow the van this weekend to watch it, but I believe the smoke is light in color.  My wife has it during the day and I work evenings, so unless I drop by her school then I'll have to wait.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||falconhulk||10-03-2001||03:43 PM||||If its blue its oil.  If its white you have a coolant problem and possibly blow head gasket  or if you have a auto trans then it could be fluid leaking on the exhaust - the latter is likely since it does not do this at startup.  Check the exhaust system for oily substances.<p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 10-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||86seyellow||10-03-2001||05:27 PM||||Dodge has had lots and lots of head gasket probs. I have goen thru 3 head gaskets on my 98 stratus. Neons and intrepids the same. It is an enginerring problem. The biggest problem with chrysler is the heads. Ask any mechanic. I would get it fixed quick before you get stranded. They arent easy at all. Thank god all of mine were under watrranty. also they came out with a new design that is rubber and metal. Only a $40 part but with labor abut $800<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||mshill||10-03-2001||06:41 PM||||By the time my 88 caravan had 99K on it, everything was falling apart, cooling system, leaked oil, burned lots of oil, transmission was about to go out for the third time.  Those Mopar 3.0L engines were terrible and the transmissions were worse than the engines.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Wolfhound||10-03-2001||07:45 PM||||I think Radar is on the right track with the vacuum modulator.||||reg||1
Z||000011||2birds||10-03-2001||10:19 PM||||I live in used mini-van hell.  Seems like all of the Chrysler (Mitsubishi) 3.0 liter sixes start to smoke around 100k.  A few real (unlike me) mechanics have told me the valve seals or guides were the culprit.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cheever3000||10-03-2001||10:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>valve seals or guides<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Sounds expensive, especially since I wouldn't be doing it myself.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||falconhulk||10-04-2001||08:59 AM||||Head gaskets on neons are not a problem.  I have had one for 7 years and had NO problems whatso ever.  Neons are the 2 liter 4 clinders anyway total diffrent engine.  Head gaskets are a good bet but if the smoke is as bad as you say I really would like to know if you have an automatic.  I know I have seen transmission leaks on the exhaust that make the car look like its on fire.  This can also occur if the transission was over filled. Its a whiteish smoke.||||reg||1
Z||000014||TSPalmer||10-04-2001||09:17 AM||||I agree on the tranny being the culprit.  The V6's are bad to have tranny probs.<P>------------------<BR>T.Shawn Palmer<BR>'85 SE 4cyl 5spd||||reg||1
Z||000015||thomas_l||10-04-2001||01:00 PM||||<B>Bad rings</B>. I believe Chrysler published a TSB on it and had a limited no-charge period, but that was years ago. The TSB basically specified a frame-in ring job and listed all the parts needed. I have seen very few 3.0L with more than 75k on them that <I>didn't</I> smoke.<P><B><I>Update:</I></B> I searched all over for more info and could only find articles about valve guides and PCV/valve cover(pre-'92) and valve stem seals. Not a thing about rings. If it's the valve stem seals that would be a lot le$$ than the rings.<p>[This message has been edited by thomas_l (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||falconhulk||10-04-2001||01:08 PM||||thomas_l thats a possiblility but the amount of smoke he describes from rings?  That would be new to me.  In order to produce that much smoke he would be going through gallons of oil.||||reg||1
Z||000017||thomas_l||10-04-2001||01:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>thomas_l thats a possiblility but the amount of smoke he describes from rings?  That would be new to me.  In order to produce that much smoke he would be going through gallons of oil.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not necessarily. The conditions that fit the ring problem were smoking during extended idling but no smoke otherwise. According to some follow up reading, bad valve stem seals would do the same on this model. On other cars valve guide/seal trouble results in smoke on start up and during & immediately following a long decel.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Cheever3000||10-04-2001||03:23 PM||||I was thinking rings myself, but I'm no expert.<P>BTW, it's an overdrive automatic transmission (I never saw a minivan that wasn't) which was replaced a year & a half ago, and I throw in a quart or two of oil into the engine about once every 3 or 4 weeks.  Sorry I forgot those points.<P>I really appreciate all the help.  I got a headlight for this vehicle through PlanetSalvage.com and will try replacing it this weekend, along with observing exactly what's happening regarding the smoke.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Cheever3000 (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||rogergarrison||10-04-2001||09:34 PM||||its very common on all Chrysler minivans, Ive had 4 and they all smoked. Its bad head design, valve guides are too soft/prone to wear. I had one that ran 95,000 miles smoking. If you dont have any other problems just live with it. If you fix it, itll just be doing it again soon anyway.||||reg||1
